I have a django template that I am including in another template. The included template has variables that need to be populated by a backend view. The view should only be called when a certain button is clicked which means that the template is rendered before it is populated by any of the view variables. How do I update the rendered template when the view is called which happens when the button is clicked. 

Some notes:
1. I don't want to use jquery so please use fetch() for the request
2. my view is currently returning the result of the render function, i.e. render(request, html\_template, context)
assistant
You can use the following code to update the rendered template when the view is called:

```
{% extends "base.html" %}

{% block content %}

{{ form.as_p }}

button type="submit" id="update-template"Update Template/button

{% endblock %}
```

```
script
document.getElementById("update-template").addEventListener("click", function() {

var request = new XMLHttpRequest();
request.open("GET", "{{ url_for('view_name') }}", true);
request.onload = function(event) {

if (request.status === 200) {

var response = request.responseText;
var template = document.getElementById("template");
template.innerHTML = response;

} else {

alert("Error loading template");

}

};
request.send();

});
/script
```

In the above code, the `update-template` button is bound to an event listener that makes a GET request to the view. The response from the view is then used to update the contents of the `template` element.

Please note that the above code is just an example. You may need to adjust it to fit your specific needs.
